<section class="page-head">
  <p class="kicker">Part IV &mdash; Tests and Essays &middot; Stress Tests &middot; Chapters 17 to 20</p>
  <h1>Where It Might Break</h1>
  <p class="lede muted">A framework is worth what its worst chapter is worth. This is that chapter, and
    in the Unified Edition it is written by the author against his own work &mdash; including one
    objection he concedes outright.</p>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--first illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 17</p>
    <h2 id="t-basel">The Infinite Poles, and the Basel Rescue</h2>
    <p class="lede">The measurement function does not have three poles. It has infinitely many: sin(πz)
      vanishes at every integer, and the ledger only works if the residues at all the distant ones
      behave.</p>
    <p>The critical question is whether the sum over those infinite poles converges. If it diverges, the
      global contour integral is undefined and the whole Ledger collapses. The residues decay as 1/n²,
      because the denominator's derivative at each distant root grows exponentially while the numerator
      stays bounded &mdash; and the sum of 1/n² is Euler's Basel series, which converges to π²/6.
      <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The imaginary parts cancel as complex-conjugate pairs; the real
      parts sum to a finite correction proportional to \(\pi^2/6\).</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\frac{1}{n^2}=\frac{\pi^2}{6}
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>The cost, stated plainly:</strong> the rescue is real but it dilutes. Those distant
      residues are not zero, so the three headline percentages are the leading terms of a sum with an
      infinite tail, not the whole of it. Any claim that the budget is exactly 68.10 / 26.95 / 4.95 has
      to answer for the tail. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    <p class="muted small">Grading: <span class="sci">&#9670; established</span> &middot;
      <span class="spec">&#9670; contested</span> &middot; <span class="myth">&#9670; vision</span>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Chapter 18 &mdash; the one the framework loses</p>
    <h2 id="t-entropy">The Entropy Objection</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p>The framework describes the cosmic energy budget as the residues of a meromorphic function with
        fixed poles. Those poles are determined by the function's definition: they do not move, interact
        or rearrange. In statistical mechanics, entropy counts the distinguishable ways a system can be
        configured while keeping its macroscopic properties &mdash; and a system with one possible
        configuration has zero entropy. Zero entropy means no thermodynamic arrow, no irreversibility, no
        explanation for why ice melts or stars burn or memory runs forward.</p>
      <p>Real physics has entropy, and a great deal of it. A black hole carries Bekenstein&ndash;Hawking
        entropy proportional to its horizon area:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        S_{\mathrm{BH}}=\frac{k_{\mathrm B}A}{4\ell_{\mathrm P}^{2}}
      \]</p>
      <p>The microwave background encodes roughly \(10^9k_{\mathrm B}\) per baryon.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <p class="lede">The honest diagnosis, in the author's own words: the static-pole picture, taken at
        face value, has no entropy and therefore no thermodynamics. This is a genuine failure &mdash; not
        a failure of vision, but a failure of the framework's current mathematical machinery to capture a
        feature the universe unarguably possesses.</p>
      <p class="muted">Three survival routes are sketched &mdash; letting the poles acquire internal
        structure, letting χ carry microstates, or treating the residues as thermodynamic averages rather
        than exact values &mdash; and each is costed. None is offered as a solution. The site keeps that
        wording, because a cosmology that announces its own failing in a numbered chapter has earned the
        right not to have it paraphrased away.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-rotation">Chapter 19 &mdash; Flat rotation curves</h3>
      <p>Galaxies rotate too fast at their edges for the visible mass to hold them together: the curve
        goes flat where Keplerian falloff is expected. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> The framework
        puts a <strong>χ-field halo</strong> in the place conventional models put a dark-matter halo
        &mdash; partially integrated matter, gravitating but dark, distributed so that the enclosed mass
        grows roughly linearly with radius.</p>
      <p class="muted">What this requires is spelt out rather than waved at: a specific radial profile
        for χ, and a reason that profile should arise rather than being fitted galaxy by galaxy. Until
        the profile is derived, this reproduces dark matter's success without yet improving on it.
        <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3 id="t-klein">Chapter 20 &mdash; Beyond the strip</h3>
      <p>If one half-twist gives a Möbius strip, what gives two? The chapter walks out to the
        <strong>Klein bottle</strong> &mdash; a closed non-orientable surface with no boundary at all
        &mdash; and then to Dyck's surface and higher genus, asking whether the universe's topology might
        be non-orientable on the largest scale.</p>
      <p class="muted">This is the one place the framework touches data it did not choose: matched-circle
        and antipodal-correlation searches in the CMB constrain such topologies.
        <span class="sci">&#9670;</span> No detection has been made. The chapter treats that as a
        boundary on the idea rather than as support for it.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">Carried over from Part I &mdash; First Principles</p>
    <h2 id="t-microtubules">The Microtubule Question, Stated Fairly</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame">
      <p>The framework sites conscious integration in the microtubule lattice, following
        Penrose&ndash;Hameroff. That proposal is neither crankery nor consensus, and the site refuses to
        present it as either.</p>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><strong>Against:</strong> Tegmark's 2000 decoherence estimate put coherence times near
          \(10^{-13}\,\mathrm{s}\) &mdash; far too short. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li><strong>In reply:</strong> Hagan, Hameroff and Tuszyński recomputed it at \(\sim10^{-4}\,\mathrm{s}\)
          in 2002. The disagreement is about the model, not the arithmetic.
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li><strong>Newer, and genuinely interesting:</strong> cooperative ultraviolet superradiance has
          been predicted and measured in mega-networks of tryptophan in cytoskeletal architectures
          (Babcock et al., 2024); the microtubule stabiliser epothilone B measurably delays anaesthetic
          loss of righting in rats (Khan et al., <em>eNeuro</em>, 2024).
          <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></li>
        <li><strong>The logical gap, named:</strong> &ldquo;we know no classical mechanism, therefore
          quantum&rdquo; is affirming the consequent. Evidence that anaesthetics act on microtubules is
          evidence about anaesthetics, not about consciousness being quantum.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The word “cannot” put under load</p>
    <h2 id="t-temporal-antipodes">The Temporal-Antipode Synchronisation Challenge</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/temporal-antipodes/">new conjecture</a> places the smallward and largeward infinities at
      opposite temporal asymptotes and says they can never fully synchronise. The intuitive sentence is clear;
      the present mathematical burden is not yet paid. An infinity is not automatically a clock, and opposite
      endpoints are not automatically physical states.</p>
    <p>Before this can count as physics, the framework must derive all four of the following without fitting
      them after the fact:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>State:</strong> identify the two phase-bearing degrees of freedom.</li>
      <li><strong>Transport:</strong> carry both to one finite comparison surface through a causal or otherwise
        explicitly justified map.</li>
      <li><strong>Criterion:</strong> preregister an interval measure of phase-and-rate locking, rather than
        calling every isolated crossing synchronisation.</li>
      <li><strong>Observable:</strong> predict a finite lag, covariance or forbidden locking pattern that
        ordinary propagation, redshift and decoherence do not already explain.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \text{opposite asymptotes}
      +\text{ no physical identification}
      +\text{ causal comparison only}
      \;\Longrightarrow\;
      \text{no complete endpoint synchrony}.
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>The clean loss:</strong> the conjecture is rejected if a consistent temporal gluing gives the
      endpoints a shared clock, if a lawful model produces exact phase-and-rate locking on a non-zero interval,
      or if its supposed signature can always be rescued by choosing a separate phase offset for each object.
      If no finite observable can be derived at all, it remains metaphysics and must not be advertised as a
      physical prediction. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame">
    <p class="kicker">The throat put under load</p>
    <h2 id="t-wormholes">The Wormhole Promotion Test</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/wormholes/">wormhole proposal</a> begins only after the inverted-torus topology has been
      specified. To count as a physical passage it must then provide a Lorentzian metric, the stress tensor
      that supports its throat, a perturbative stability analysis, a causal evolution for both mouths and a
      signal distinguishable from ordinary lensing or a compact object.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      \text{orientation map}
      \;\not\Longrightarrow\;
      \text{traversable throat};
      \qquad
      (g_{\mu\nu},T_{\mu\nu},\text{stability},\text{causality})
      \;\text{are still required}.
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>The clean loss:</strong> withdraw the physical wormhole reading if no self-consistent source
      holds the throat open, if arbitrarily small perturbations destroy it, if chronology cannot be made
      consistent, or if every proposed observation is already supplied by a black hole, lens or coordinate
      relabelling. The global mapping-torus model may survive that loss as topology; the portal claim may not.
      <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

