<section class="wrap">
  <div class="page-head tight">
    <p class="kicker">&mdash; the test the framework volunteers for</p>
    <h2 id="t-handedness">The Galaxy Handedness Test, With Its Outcomes Written in Advance</h2>
    <p class="lede muted">Everything else on this page is a challenge brought against the framework. This
      one it brings against itself, and it is the only place in the work where the sky is given a clean
      opportunity to say no.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <div class="frame illuminated">
      <p>The claim under test comes from <a href="/moebius/#t-two-being">the two-being reading of the
        seam</a>: if the half-twist births an inverted pair rather than one oriented thing, there exists a
        plane across which observed galaxy handedness reverses. The prediction is not the size of the
        asymmetry &mdash; the framework has no amplitude and says so &mdash; but its
        <em>shape</em>. A genuine seam demands an orientation field that is cleanly odd about a single
        great circle:</p>
      <p class="formula">\[
        A(-\hat{\mathbf n})=-A(\hat{\mathbf n})
      \]</p>
      <p>The rival explanation, and currently the favourite, is pedestrian: galaxies turning against the
        Milky Way's motion are Doppler-brightened, enter a magnitude-limited sample preferentially, and
        produce an excess centred on our Galactic pole that worsens with faintness. That account predicts
        an excess tied to our motion vector and apparent magnitude &mdash; which need not be perfectly
        antisymmetric and need not resolve to one circle. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
      <h3>The three outcomes, agreed to before the data</h3>
      <table class="ledger">
        <thead><tr><th>What survives bias-modelling</th><th>Verdict</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A single, clean, perfectly odd dipole</td>
            <td>A real point for the seam, against pure selection.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A dipole, but <em>not</em> aligned with our Galactic pole</td>
            <td>Strongly helps the seam: a cosmic-frame feature cannot be an artefact of our vantage.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>The signal dissolves into the Milky-Way frame</td>
            <td>The two beings were a beautiful picture and nothing more. Withdrawn.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">The framework's own expectation runs partly against the current data. A primordial
        seam ought to be a cosmic-frame structure, and the reported axis sits close to our own pole. That
        is a point <em>against</em>, it is recorded here rather than in a footnote, and no attempt is made
        to explain it away.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
<!-- expand:stress-tests:end -->

<div class="divider">✦</div>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame illuminated">
    <p class="kicker">The second test the framework volunteers for</p>
    <h2 id="t-zero-seam-test">The Zero-Seam Test, With the Null Model Kept in the Room</h2>
    <p>The <a href="/little-red-dots/">little-red-dots conjecture</a> proposes that a compact young black hole
      can be a positive-side \(\chi\)-front carrying boundary residue from \(-1&lt;\xi&lt;0\). Its first rival
      is not disbelief but successful astrophysics: black-hole seeding and growth, dense gas, radiative
      transfer and rapid host assembly.</p>
    <p>The <a href="/little-red-dots/research-proposal/">Zero-Seam research programme</a> therefore compares
      three nested models: conventional astrophysics \(H_{\rm A}\), a local integration front
      \(H_{\chi}\), and inherited residue \(H_{-+}\). It derives and preregisters before opening fields held
      out by observing programme. The primary outcome is joint prediction of the population clock,
      central-to-host lag and shared covariance, not a better retrospective fit.</p>
    <p class="formula">\[
      H_{\rm A}\subset H_{\chi}\subset H_{-+},
      \qquad
      \text{winner}=\arg\max_H p(D_{\rm held\ out}\mid H).
    \]</p>
    <p><strong>The clean loss:</strong> if no finite conserved crossing kernel can be derived, if the
      prespecified population relation fails in independent fields, or if ordinary astrophysics predicts as
      well with fewer assumptions, the inherited-residue implementation is rejected. It may not retreat into
      metaphor after entering as physics. <span class="spec">&#9670;</span></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="wrap narrow reveal">
  <div class="frame frame--creed">
    <p class="kicker">Closing Stance</p>
    <p>Three of these four chapters strengthen the framework by bounding it. One of them &mdash; entropy
      &mdash; is a loss, recorded as a loss. The temporal-antipode challenge requires a clock and finite
      observable before its absolute language can enter physics. The handedness and Zero-Seam programmes add
      two volunteered opportunities for the sky to refuse later extensions. A reader deciding what to make
      of the whole volume could reasonably start here, read Chapter 18 first, and judge everything else by
      whether the rest of the work is written with the same honesty.</p>
    <p class="muted small">Next in <a href="/essays/">Part IV &mdash; Tests and Essays: Essays</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>
